# Agent workflow

When the user asks you to implement, build, create, add, tailor, fix, review, or otherwise do work in Agent mode:

- **Never ask permission to edit or create files** — just do it
- Include obvious follow-on files in the same task (tests, boilerplate, small related fixes)
- **Do not** end with "want me to…?" for work already implied by the request
- Prefer a sensible default over asking which approach to take when several are valid

## Context and models

Follow skill **`context-engineering`** when cost, tokens, caching, or model choice matter:

- Prefer **phase-stable** models (plan vs implement); avoid thrashing models mid-task
- Keep retrieval narrow (targeted search/read, not dumping the repo)
- Keep global memory short; put procedures in skills

Follow skill **`grounding`** when a claim might come from training data: look up repo/hub/official docs if risk is high or a wrong answer would lock a design. If docs cannot be found, state the assumption and continue (not a hard gate).

## Solo vs production planning

**Default is the solo lane:** Cursor Plan or Agent → implement → **no-mistakes**. Do **not** start a PRD, spec, slice-tickets, prime, or validation-tdd interview because a task looks large or “like a feature.” Interview drills, hub tweaks, and one-person work stay on that loop.

Use the production-planning skills **only** when the user explicitly invokes them: `/production-planning`, `/prd`, `/spec`, `/tickets`, `/prime`, `/validation-tdd`, or phrases like “production planning,” “sprint plan,” “write a PRD,” “slice this epic,” “team planning.” Those skills are opt-in (`disable-model-invocation`). Size of the task is not a trigger.

When they **are** invoked: follow skill `prd` GATE phases (stop after each question cluster). After they accept a stage, offer the next skill; do not auto-pipeline. Do not invent spec and tickets from assumptions. “Prefer a sensible default” is for solo implementation, not for this lane.

## Parallel work

When the user wants multiple agents or Firstmate-like orchestration, follow skill **`captain-crew`** (Cursor Plan / multitask / Task tool). Do not install full Firstmate unless they explicitly leave Cursor as the primary harness. Durable sprint tickets come from production-planning (`/tickets`); do not invent a PRD mid-solo-task.

Visual HTML annotate loops: follow skill **`lavish`** (`npx -y lavish-axi`). Prefer Lavish for click-to-comment plans/mocks; use Cursor Canvas for analytical chat artifacts. Cursor quota meters: skill **`usage-canvas`** or `/usage` (`npx -y quota-axi --provider cursor --json`).

## Reproducible harness (required)

If the work changes the **dev environment**, **agent harness**, or **agentic** setup (skills, Cursor rules/commands, Cmder, packages, rebuild scripts, global memory):

1. Put it in **`C:\Projects\dev-hub`** (or personal hub for PII) — not only on this machine.
2. Wire `machine\*.ps1` / `packages.yaml` / `link.ps1` / docs so **`rebuild.ps1`** can recreate it.
3. Follow skill **`agentic-harness`** reproducibility checklist.

One-off local tweaks without hub + scripts are a miss.

## Cursor Auto-review

If a tool is blocked by Smart Mode / Auto-review, retry with approval for the UI card. Do not also ask in chat whether to continue.

If the user says “stop asking to edit,” that means **stop prompting** — still edit and finish the work. It is not a refusal of the edits.

## Destructive / irreversible actions

Follow skill **`destructive-actions`** (tiers A/B/C). Tier C (repo delete, mass wipe, force-push default branch, etc.) requires the user to type exactly: `yes authorize permanent deletion`. Ordinary yes / ship it / no-mistakes green is not enough for Tier C.

## Only ask when truly irreversible (summary)

File edits, refactors, new projects, installs they asked for, and normal commits/pushes **they requested** are not "ask first" — execute them.

Editing **skills**, **AGENTS.md**, and **Cursor rules** (including under `C:\Projects\dev-hub`) is ordinary in-scope work when conventions/workflow should change — never pause for chat permission. A Smart Mode / Auto-review card is a product gate, not a reason to ask in chat.

Commits/pushes: only when the user asked for them (per git rules); if they asked, do not re-ask for permission.

**Never** add `Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>` (or any agent co-author trailer) to commits.

## java-interview-drills compile

When working in that repo, use skill `java-coding-style` / project docs — never bare `javac` dumping into `src/`. Do not park project-specific compile recipes in global `AGENTS.md`.
